WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.100
Good morning Hank, it's Tuesday

00:00:02.100 --> 00:00:04.900
and this is the first chapter of Turtles All the Way Down

00:00:04.900 --> 00:00:06.400
which I'll be reading to you today.

00:00:06.400 --> 00:00:11.180
Okay, I think all you need to know is that this story is narrated by a sixteen-year-old girl named Aza Holmes

00:00:11.180 --> 00:00:13.220
Also it starts with a--with an epigraph

00:00:13.220 --> 00:00:14.520
from Arthur Schopenhauer:

00:00:14.760 --> 00:00:16.440
"Man can do what he wills,

00:00:16.440 --> 00:00:18.640
but he cannot will what he wills."

00:00:18.780 --> 00:00:19.920
Chapter One.

00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:22.220
At the time I first realized I might be fictional,

00:00:22.220 --> 00:00:26.420
my weekdays were spent at a publicly funded institution on the north side of Indianapolis

00:00:26.420 --> 00:00:27.880
called White River High School,

00:00:27.880 --> 00:00:30.660
where I was required to eat lunch at a particular time--

00:00:30.780 --> 00:00:33.060
between 12:37 and 1:14 P.M.--

00:00:33.060 --> 00:00:35.360
by forces so much larger than myself

00:00:35.360 --> 00:00:37.780
that I couldn't even begin to identify them.

00:00:37.780 --> 00:00:40.480
If those forces had given me a different lunch period,

00:00:40.480 --> 00:00:42.860
or if the tablemates who helped author my fate

00:00:42.860 --> 00:00:46.100
had chosen a different topic of conversation that September day,

00:00:46.100 --> 00:00:47.780
I would've met a different end

00:00:47.780 --> 00:00:49.240
--or at least a different middle.

00:00:49.240 --> 00:00:52.780
But I was beginning to learn that your life is a story told about you,

00:00:52.860 --> 00:00:54.300
not one that you tell.

00:00:54.300 --> 00:00:56.720
Of course, you pretend to be the author.

00:00:56.940 --> 00:00:57.740
You have to.

00:00:58.580 --> 00:00:59.460
You think,

00:00:59.460 --> 00:01:01.160
'I now choose to go to lunch,'

00:01:01.160 --> 00:01:04.440
when that monotone beep rings from on high at 12:37.

00:01:04.540 --> 00:01:06.540
But really, the bell chooses.

00:01:06.540 --> 00:01:09.260
You think you're the painter, but you're the canvas.

00:01:09.900 --> 00:01:13.080
Hundreds of voices were shouting over one another in the cafeteria,

00:01:13.080 --> 00:01:15.560
so that the conversation became mere sound,

00:01:15.560 --> 00:01:17.700
the rushing of a river over rocks.

00:01:18.140 --> 00:01:20.140
And as I sat beneath fluorescent cylinders

00:01:20.140 --> 00:01:22.140
spewing aggressively artificial light,

00:01:22.140 --> 00:01:26.480
I thought about how we all believed ourselves to be the hero of some personal epic,

00:01:26.480 --> 00:01:29.740
when in fact we were basically identical organisms

00:01:29.740 --> 00:01:32.300
colonizing a vast and windowless room

00:01:32.380 --> 00:01:34.460
that smelled of Lysol and lard.

00:01:34.460 --> 00:01:38.280
I was eating a peanut butter and honey sandwich and drinking a Dr Pepper.

00:01:38.280 --> 00:01:42.000
To be honest, I find the whole process of masticating plants and animals

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:44.820
and then shoving them down my esophagus kind of disgusting,

00:01:44.820 --> 00:01:47.720
so I was trying not to think about the fact that I was eating,

00:01:47.720 --> 00:01:49.260
which is a form of thinking about it.

00:01:49.480 --> 00:01:51.160
Across the table from me,

00:01:51.160 --> 00:01:54.160
Mychal Turner was scribbling in a yellow-paper notebook.

00:01:54.160 --> 00:01:57.180
Our lunch table was like a long-running play on Broadway:

00:01:57.180 --> 00:01:59.280
The cast changed some over the years,

00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:00.500
but the roles never did.

00:02:00.800 --> 00:02:02.480
Mychal was The Artsy One.

00:02:02.480 --> 00:02:04.480
He was talking with Daisy Ramirez,

00:02:04.480 --> 00:02:07.980
who'd played the role of my Best and Most Fearless Friend since elementary school,

00:02:07.980 --> 00:02:11.720
but I couldn't follow their conversation over the noise of all the others.

00:02:11.720 --> 00:02:13.420
What was my part in this play?

00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:14.760
The Sidekick.

00:02:16.320 --> 00:02:17.760
I was Daisy's Friend,

00:02:17.760 --> 00:02:19.360
or Ms. Holmes's Daughter.

00:02:19.360 --> 00:02:21.140
I was somebody's something.

00:02:21.140 --> 00:02:23.840
I felt my stomach begin to work on the sandwich,

00:02:23.840 --> 00:02:25.600
and even over everybody's talking,

00:02:25.680 --> 00:02:27.440
I could hear it digesting,

00:02:27.440 --> 00:02:30.540
all the bacteria chewing the slime of peanut butter

00:02:30.540 --> 00:02:34.160
--the students inside of me eating at my internal cafeteria.

00:02:34.260 --> 00:02:36.340
A shiver convulsed through me.

00:02:36.340 --> 00:02:38.980
"Didn't you go to camp with him?" Daisy asked me.

00:02:39.440 --> 00:02:39.940
"With who?"

00:02:40.060 --> 00:02:41.900
"Davis Pickett," she said.

00:02:42.460 --> 00:02:44.380
"Yeah," I said. "Why?"

00:02:44.400 --> 00:02:46.960
"Aren't you listening?" Daisy asked.

00:02:47.260 --> 00:02:51.580
I am listening, I thought, to the cacophony of my digestive tract.

00:02:51.580 --> 00:02:56.460
Of course I'd long known that I was playing host to a massive collection of parasitic organisms,

00:02:56.460 --> 00:02:58.720
but I didn't much like being reminded of it.

00:02:58.720 --> 00:03:02.700
By cell count, humans are approximately 50 percent microbial,

00:03:02.700 --> 00:03:06.920
meaning that about half of the cells that make you up are not yours at all.

00:03:06.920 --> 00:03:09.780
There are something like a thousand times more microbes

00:03:09.780 --> 00:03:13.600
living in my particular biome than there are human beings on earth,

00:03:13.740 --> 00:03:19.560
and it often seems like I can feel them living and breeding and dying in and on me.

00:03:20.060 --> 00:03:22.300
I wiped my sweaty palms on my jeans

00:03:22.300 --> 00:03:23.920
and tried to control my breathing.

00:03:23.920 --> 00:03:26.320
Admittedly, I have some anxiety problems,

00:03:26.320 --> 00:03:31.860
but I would argue it isn't irrational to be concerned about the fact that you are a skin-encased bacterial colony.

00:03:31.860 --> 00:03:34.760
Mychal said, "His dad was about to be arrested for bribery or something,

00:03:34.760 --> 00:03:36.460
but the night before the raid he disappeared.

00:03:36.460 --> 00:03:38.860
There's a hundred-thousand-dollar reward out for him."

00:03:39.120 --> 00:03:41.440
"And you know his kid," Daisy said.

00:03:41.720 --> 00:03:43.320
"Knew him," I answered.

00:03:43.320 --> 00:03:48.140
I watched Daisy attack her school-provided rectangular pizza and green beans with a fork.

00:03:48.140 --> 00:03:52.200
She kept glancing up at me, her eyes widening as if to say, 'Well?'

00:03:52.200 --> 00:03:54.600
I could tell she wanted me to ask her about something,

00:03:54.600 --> 00:03:55.960
but I couldn't tell what,

00:03:55.960 --> 00:03:57.220
because my stomach wouldn't shut up,

00:03:57.220 --> 00:04:02.040
which was forcing me deep inside a worry that I'd somehow contracted a parasitic infection.

00:04:02.040 --> 00:04:05.680
I could half hear Mychal telling Daisy about his new art project,

00:04:05.680 --> 00:04:09.740
in which he was using Photoshop to average the faces of a hundred people named Mychal,

00:04:09.740 --> 00:04:13.380
and the average of their faces would be this new, one-hundred-and-first Mychal,

00:04:13.380 --> 00:04:15.760
and it was an interesting idea, and I wanted to listen,

00:04:15.760 --> 00:04:17.680
but the cafeteria was so loud,

00:04:17.680 --> 00:04:22.900
and I couldn't stop wondering whether there was something wrong with the microbial balance of power inside me.

00:04:22.900 --> 00:04:26.700
Excessive abdominal noise is an uncommon, but not unprecedented,

00:04:26.700 --> 00:04:30.440
presenting symptom of an infection with the bacteria Clostridium Difficile,

00:04:30.460 --> 00:04:31.740
which can be fatal.

00:04:31.740 --> 00:04:34.940
I pulled out my phone and searched "human microbiome"

00:04:34.940 --> 00:04:39.760
to reread Wikipedia's introduction to the trillions of microorganisms currently inside me.

00:04:39.760 --> 00:04:41.680
I clicked over to the article about C. diff,

00:04:41.680 --> 00:04:44.980
scrolling to the part about how most C. diff infections occur in hospitals.

00:04:44.980 --> 00:04:47.300
I scrolled down farther to a list of symptoms,

00:04:47.300 --> 00:04:48.260
none of which I had,

00:04:48.260 --> 00:04:50.080
except for the excessive abdominal noises,

00:04:50.080 --> 00:04:51.640
although I knew from previous searches

00:04:51.640 --> 00:04:55.460
that the Cleveland Clinic had reported the case of one person who'd died of C. diff

00:04:55.460 --> 00:04:58.940
after presenting at the hospital with only abdominal pain and fever.

00:04:59.140 --> 00:05:01.480
I reminded myself that I didn't have a fever,

00:05:01.480 --> 00:05:02.800
and my self replied:

00:05:02.800 --> 00:05:04.320
You don't have a fever YET.

00:05:04.320 --> 00:05:08.080
At the cafeteria, where a shrinking slice of my consciousness still resided,

00:05:08.080 --> 00:05:11.900
Daisy was telling Mychal that his averaging project shouldn't be about people named Mychal

00:05:11.900 --> 00:05:14.460
but about imprisoned men who'd later been exonerated.

00:05:14.460 --> 00:05:16.320
"It'll be easier, anyway," she said,

00:05:16.320 --> 00:05:18.940
"because they all have mug shots taken from the same angle,

00:05:18.940 --> 00:05:23.220
and then it's not just about names but also about race and class and mass incarceration,"

00:05:23.220 --> 00:05:25.580
and Mychal was like, "You're a genius, Daisy,"

00:05:25.580 --> 00:05:27.460
and she said, "You sound surprised,"

00:05:27.460 --> 00:05:31.540
and meanwhile I was thinking that if half the cells inside of you are not you,

00:05:31.540 --> 00:05:35.680
doesn't that challenge the whole notion of 'me' as a singular pronoun,

00:05:35.680 --> 00:05:37.480
let alone as the author of my fate?

00:05:37.480 --> 00:05:40.540
And I fell pretty far down that recursive wormhole

00:05:40.540 --> 00:05:44.000
until it transported me completely out of the White River High School cafeteria

00:05:44.000 --> 00:05:48.340
into some non-sensorial place only properly crazy people get to visit.

00:05:48.960 --> 00:05:50.180
Ever since I was little,

00:05:50.180 --> 00:05:53.680
I've pressed my right thumbnail into the finger pad of my middle finger,

00:05:53.680 --> 00:05:57.020
and so now there's this weird callus over my fingerprint.

00:05:57.020 --> 00:06:01.720
After so many years of doing this, I can open up a crack in the skin really easily,

00:06:01.720 --> 00:06:04.680
so I cover it up with a Band-Aid to try to prevent infection.

00:06:04.680 --> 00:06:07.160
But sometimes I get worried that there already is an infection,

00:06:07.160 --> 00:06:08.160
and so I need to drain it,

00:06:08.160 --> 00:06:10.380
and the only way to do that is to reopen the wound

00:06:10.380 --> 00:06:12.100
and press out any blood that will come.

00:06:12.100 --> 00:06:14.720
Once I start thinking about splitting the skin apart,

00:06:14.900 --> 00:06:17.120
I literally cannot not do it.

00:06:17.120 --> 00:06:21.380
I apologize for the double negative, but it's a real double negative of a situation,

00:06:21.380 --> 00:06:24.760
a bind from which negating the negation is the only escape.

00:06:25.260 --> 00:06:29.780
So anyway, I started to want to feel my thumbnail biting into the skin of my finger pad,

00:06:29.780 --> 00:06:32.460
and I knew that resistance was more or less futile,

00:06:32.460 --> 00:06:34.280
so beneath the cafeteria table,

00:06:34.280 --> 00:06:39.380
I slipped the Band-Aid off my finger and dug my thumbnail into the calloused skin until I felt it crack open.

00:06:39.380 --> 00:06:40.940
"Holmesy," Daisy said.

00:06:40.940 --> 00:06:42.140
I looked up at her.

00:06:42.140 --> 00:06:44.860
"We're almost through lunch and you haven't even mentioned my hair."

00:06:44.860 --> 00:06:48.000
She shook out her hair, with so-red-they-were-pink highlights.

00:06:48.160 --> 00:06:50.160
Right. She'd dyed her hair.

00:06:50.320 --> 00:06:53.520
I swum up out of the depths and said, "It's bold."

00:06:53.620 --> 00:06:54.660
"I know, right?

00:06:54.660 --> 00:06:58.820
It says, 'Ladies and gentlemen and also people who do not identify as ladies or gentlemen,

00:06:58.820 --> 00:07:02.300
Daisy Ramirez won't break her promises, but she will break your heart'"

00:07:02.300 --> 00:07:06.120
Daisy's self-proclaimed life motto was "Break Hearts, Not Promises."

00:07:06.120 --> 00:07:09.620
She kept threatening to get it tattooed on her ankle when she turned eighteen.

00:07:09.620 --> 00:07:11.520
Daisy turned back to Mychal,

00:07:11.520 --> 00:07:12.760
and I to my thoughts.

00:07:12.760 --> 00:07:16.260
The stomach grumbling had grown, if anything, louder.

00:07:16.440 --> 00:07:18.120
I felt like I might vomit.

00:07:18.120 --> 00:07:20.820
For somebody who actively dislikes bodily fluids,

00:07:20.820 --> 00:07:22.200
I throw up quite a lot.

00:07:22.200 --> 00:07:24.020
"Holmesy, you okay?" Daisy asked.

00:07:24.460 --> 00:07:25.180
I nodded.

00:07:25.180 --> 00:07:28.740
Sometimes I wondered why she liked me, or at least tolerated me.

00:07:28.940 --> 00:07:30.220
Why any of them did.

00:07:30.360 --> 00:07:32.360
Even I found myself annoying.

00:07:32.360 --> 00:07:37.180
I could feel sweat sprouting from my forehead, and once I begin to sweat, it's impossible to stop.

00:07:37.180 --> 00:07:38.540
I'll keep sweating for hours,

00:07:38.540 --> 00:07:40.500
and not just my face or my armpits.

00:07:40.500 --> 00:07:43.360
My neck sweats. My boobs sweat. My calves sweat.

00:07:43.420 --> 00:07:45.020
Maybe I did have a fever.

00:07:45.020 --> 00:07:48.000
Beneath the table, I slid the old Band-Aid into my pocket

00:07:48.000 --> 00:07:50.380
and, without looking, pulled out a new one, unwrapped it,

00:07:50.380 --> 00:07:52.420
and then glanced down to apply it to my finger.

00:07:52.420 --> 00:07:56.080
All the while, I was breathing in through my nose and out through my mouth,

00:07:56.080 --> 00:07:58.280
in the manner advised by Dr. Karen Singh,

00:07:58.280 --> 00:08:01.940
exhaling at a pace "that would make a candle flicker but not go out.

00:08:01.940 --> 00:08:03.380
Imagine that candle, Aza,

00:08:03.380 --> 00:08:06.640
flickering from your breath but still there, always there."

00:08:06.900 --> 00:08:07.940
So I tried that,

00:08:07.940 --> 00:08:10.520
but the thought spiral kept tightening anyway.

00:08:10.520 --> 00:08:13.280
I could hear Dr. Singh saying I shouldn't get out my phone,

00:08:13.280 --> 00:08:15.880
that I mustn't look up the same questions over and over,

00:08:15.880 --> 00:08:17.240
but I got it out anyway,

00:08:17.240 --> 00:08:20.180
and reread the "Human Microbiota" Wikipedia article.

00:08:20.180 --> 00:08:21.960
The thing about a spiral is,

00:08:22.040 --> 00:08:23.640
if you follow it inward,

00:08:24.080 --> 00:08:25.680
it never actually ends.

00:08:26.040 --> 00:08:28.820
It just keeps tightening, infinitely.

00:08:28.820 --> 00:08:32.320
I sealed the Ziploc bag around the last quarter of my sandwich,

00:08:32.320 --> 00:08:34.980
got up, and tossed it into an overfilled trash can.

00:08:34.980 --> 00:08:36.960
I heard a voice from behind me.

00:08:36.960 --> 00:08:41.220
"How concerned should I be that you haven't said more than two words in a row all day?"

00:08:41.220 --> 00:08:43.720
"Thought spiral," I mumbled in reply.

00:08:43.720 --> 00:08:45.720
Daisy had known me since we were six,

00:08:45.720 --> 00:08:46.920
long enough to get it.

00:08:46.920 --> 00:08:49.680
"I figured. Sorry, man. Let's hang out today."

00:08:49.680 --> 00:08:52.580
This girl Molly walked up to us, smiling, and said,

00:08:52.580 --> 00:08:56.400
"Uh, Daisy, just FYI, your Kool-Aid dye job is staining your shirt."

00:08:56.400 --> 00:09:01.140
Daisy looked down at her shoulders, and indeed, her striped top had turned pink in spots.

00:09:01.140 --> 00:09:02.880
She flinched for a second,

00:09:02.880 --> 00:09:04.400
then straightened her spine.

00:09:04.400 --> 00:09:08.100
"Yeah, it's part of the look, Molly. Stained shirts are huge in Paris right now."

00:09:08.100 --> 00:09:10.120
She turned away from Molly and said,

00:09:10.120 --> 00:09:13.040
"Right, so we'll go to your house and watch Star Wars: Rebels."

00:09:13.040 --> 00:09:17.100
Daisy was really into Star Wars--and not just the movies, but also the books

00:09:17.100 --> 00:09:20.440
and the animated shows and the kids' show where they're all made out of Lego.

00:09:20.440 --> 00:09:23.160
Like, she wrote fan fiction about Chewbacca's love life.

00:09:23.160 --> 00:09:28.640
"And we will improve your mood until you are able to say three or even four words in a row; sound good?"

00:09:28.920 --> 00:09:29.960
"Sounds good."

00:09:29.960 --> 00:09:32.800
"And then you can take me to work. Sorry, but I need a ride."

00:09:33.260 --> 00:09:33.820
"Okay."

00:09:33.940 --> 00:09:38.900
I wanted to say more, but the thoughts kept coming, unbidden and unwanted.

00:09:39.100 --> 00:09:40.620
If I'd been the author,

00:09:40.620 --> 00:09:43.080
I would've stopped thinking about my microbiome.

00:09:43.080 --> 00:09:46.800
I would've told Daisy how much I liked her idea for Mychal's art project,

00:09:46.800 --> 00:09:49.680
and I would've told her that I did remember Davis Pickett,

00:09:49.680 --> 00:09:53.660
that I remembered being eleven and carrying a vague but constant fear.

00:09:53.660 --> 00:09:58.120
I would've told her that I remembered once at camp lying next to Davis on the edge of a dock,

00:09:58.120 --> 00:09:59.500
our legs dangling over,

00:09:59.500 --> 00:10:02.080
our backs against the rough-hewn planks of wood,

00:10:02.080 --> 00:10:04.700
staring together up at a cloudless summer sky.

00:10:04.700 --> 00:10:07.380
I would've told her that Davis and I never talked much,

00:10:07.380 --> 00:10:08.700
or even looked at each other,

00:10:08.700 --> 00:10:10.100
but it didn't matter,

00:10:10.100 --> 00:10:15.480
because we were looking at the same sky together, which is maybe more intimate than eye contact anyway.

00:10:15.840 --> 00:10:17.440
Anybody can look at you.

00:10:17.440 --> 00:10:20.880
It's quite rare to find someone who sees the same world you see.

00:10:21.820 --> 00:10:22.460
Thanks for listening!

00:10:22.460 --> 00:10:23.880
I'm not gonna lie, I'm very nervous,

00:10:23.880 --> 00:10:25.240
but I hope that you liked it.

00:10:25.240 --> 00:10:27.560
If you want to preorder the book, there are links below.

00:10:27.560 --> 00:10:29.240
Hank, I will see you on Friday.

